Z||000000||Greg Piet||12-18-2000||08:30 PM||||Ok after a season of struggling with my Yokohama S306 street tires I got an amazing set of used tires(20-25% worn for $70 each).<BR>Hooser A3S03 in 205/50/ZR15 ($160 each new).<P>Now I am just itching to take them out on the track, and see how the car does with tires designed for autocrossing.<P>Hi my name is Greg and I am a autocrossholic.||||reg||6
Z||000001||Cheever2||12-18-2000||09:00 PM||||I don't have anything to cross the auto with.  But I'm thinking about joining the SCCA just to be involved with their events, especially the CART Houston G.P.  Whaddaya think?<P>||||reg||6
Z||000002||Greg Piet||12-19-2000||03:58 PM||||Yes join up with teh scca!<BR>Everyone I have met are realy nice.<BR>Those cart-ers are pretty crazy in my mind, but it looks fun as heck!<P>Hironaka who just wont the F-125 (carts) at nationals is in the same region as I, and every time we  go out, he keeps trying to convince me to switch over to a cart.<P>If you just want to do flagging and stuff at races that is an excelelnt way to be really up close to the races, and it free.  The worst thing is that you have to watch the cars go fast...  oh wait that is why you came out in the first place, nevermind, I guess it rules!||||reg||1
Z||000003||PearlBlueSoul||12-19-2000||04:09 PM||||man, I wana drive an Atlantic car.  those are schweet!  tuned up version of my MR2 motor with 240 n/a HP and 12k RPM.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <P>I'd also like to drive in SCCA.  Just, am kind of confused on how.  I've got some research to do yet.  <P>------------------<BR>1987 Toyota MR2 N/A 5 speed<BR>  <A HREF="http://www.atlantamr2s.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.atlantamr2s.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/darionscard</A>  <BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard/Thumb.jpg">        <BR><B>Notable mods:</B>  Sus tech springs and sway bars, Toyota strut tower brace, KYB strut inserts, HKS exhaust, NGK plugs/wires, Momo Racer wheels (14x6), Custom intake, Kenwood 12" speaker in front trunk, Jensen amp.<p>[This message has been edited by PearlBlueSoul (edited 12-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cheever2||12-19-2000||06:16 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Greg Piet:<BR><B>If you just want to do flagging and stuff at races </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE>Yeah, that's what I thought would get me closest to the cars in the shortest time.  They pair up an experienced person with a lesser experienced one.  This year the volunteers got some free stuff too.<P>When I said CART, I meant as in <B>C</B>hampionship <B>A</B>uto <B>R</B>acing <B>T</B>eams.  Best example:<BR>- <A HREF="http://www.teamcheever.com" TARGET=_blank>www.teamcheever.com</A> <BR>-<BR>Go Eddie!  Cheever Fever!<P>||||reg||6
Z||000005||Greg Piet||12-19-2000||11:12 PM||||FYI:<P>From my racing buds... Alot of those pro drivers who run open wheels, typically drive go-karts to keep their skills sharp, and they are amazingly similiar as far as responsiveness and vehicle behavior...<BR>Something about going a buck thirty in a go kart makes me believe my fellow racers who do the go kart thing are not lying about racing against pro drivers on the go kart tracks.||||reg||1
